#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124288 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a568fc63-e81c-5210-93f0-bcbd429a9e40" :question/math-academy-id "e-6678" :question/problem "A biologist observed that the growth of the number of fish in a lake is directly proportional to the difference between the lake’s maximum fish population and the current population. Let $P(t)$ be the fish population, measured in number of fish, and let $t \\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum fish population that the lake can support is $200$ fish, which differential equation could be used to model the lake’s fish population? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the fish population $P(t)$ of the lake changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum fish population the lake can support and the current fish population. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t} = k(a - P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum fish population the lake can support, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a = 200 \\textrm{ fish},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t} = k(200 - P).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124289 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d603efdd-6e27-5e14-a106-f9fc4a182403" :question/math-academy-id "e-6679" :question/problem "In an aquarium, there are initially $20$ fish. After $30$ weeks, the number of fish is $50.$ Assuming inhibited growth, calculate the number of fish after $100$ weeks if the maximum capacity of the aquarium is $90$ fish." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of fish $N(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the total number of fish and the current number of fish. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}N}{\\mathrm{d}t}=k\\left(a-N\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the aquarium, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a = 90$ fish, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d} N}{\\mathrm{d} t} = k(90-N).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dN}{90-N} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dN}{90-N} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|90-N| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $90 - N > 0$ for all $t \\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $N(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}90-N & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 90-N & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 90-N & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ N & =90-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^{-C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial number of fish was $20.$ So, we apply the initial condition $N(0)=20,$ and solve for $K{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =20 \\\\ 90-K{e}^{0} & =20 \\\\ 90-K & =20 \\\\ K & =70\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $N(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nN(t)=90 - 70 e^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $30$ weeks, the number of fish is $50.$ So, applying the condition $N(30)=50,$ we can solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(30) & =50 \\\\ 90-70{e}^{-30k} & =50 \\\\ 40 & =70{e}^{-30k} \\\\ {e}^{-30k} & =\\frac{4}{7} \\\\ -30k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{7}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{30}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{7})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nN(t)=90-70e^{(t/30)\\,\\ln\\left(4/7\\right)}.\n$$\n\nFinally,  the number of fish after $t=100$ weeks is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(100) & =90-70{e}^{(100/30)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4/7)} \\\\  & =79.161… \\\\  & ≈79\\,\\text{fish},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124291 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ade69467-c229-509d-9bdc-b28935b2d1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Growth Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d178c83a-b8af-5d6a-bf40-fcfb7506feeb" :question/math-academy-id "e-6686" :question/problem "Given that $y \\lt 60$ and $t \\ge 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 240 - 4y$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4y - 240$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4y^2 - 240$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4 - 240y$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = k(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ for all $t \\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 240 - 4y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4(60-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=4,$ and $a=60.$ Note that for inhibited growth, we must have $y \\lt 60$ for all $t \\ge 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124681 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ade69467-c229-509d-9bdc-b28935b2d1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Growth Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd57bce4-bf4d-5488-ada6-93439471fb78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 5y^{2} - 400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121106} {:answer/id #uuid "1f00b80a-9440-52ad-bb3e-9a6954010114" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 5 - 400y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121107} {:answer/id #uuid "34a003e9-7090-500a-bcd5-5a07bde1c2a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 400 - 5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121108} {:answer/id #uuid "35a91da3-7b8e-54e8-8314-4340d804eddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 5y - 400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121109} {:answer/id #uuid "6623a9f2-ffe7-561f-9984-2a59cbb33aa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 400 - 5y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34a003e9-7090-500a-bcd5-5a07bde1c2a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 400 - 5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121108} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4164cc7-8b43-5b53-b800-89f7a6270478" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "092a8c01-7fc1-532c-ba20-9cb4c823a717" :question/math-academy-id "q-106662" :question/problem "Given that $y<80$ and $t\\ge 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=400-5y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=5(80-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=5,$ and $a=80.$ Note that for inhibited growth, we must have $y<80$ for all $t\\ge 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ade69467-c229-509d-9bdc-b28935b2d1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Growth Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd2b0d2f-278e-5f3a-872b-4c55db1063e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 3(250 - y), y(0) = 150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121112} {:answer/id #uuid "d93318fe-144b-54f6-ad46-9f78e39caca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} =-3(y + 250), y(0) = 100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121113} {:answer/id #uuid "118e1393-433d-5237-b2ec-5ca7c22512e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 3(250 - y), y(0) = 400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121114} {:answer/id #uuid "33cf5133-7c8a-5eac-b8a8-3fa513d8038e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 3y(y - 250), y(0) = 50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121115} {:answer/id #uuid "5ea36dc2-bfcf-5d09-b596-d2ace383757e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 3(y - 250), y(0) = 200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd2b0d2f-278e-5f3a-872b-4c55db1063e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 3(250 - y), y(0) = 150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121112} :answer-field/id #uuid "40ee9563-8785-53ef-b91d-f1c369b24a3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8f97edbb-38b9-5a3e-9db9-d7a29ce3a4af" :question/math-academy-id "q-106663" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited growth problem?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=3(250-y),\\,y(0)=150.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=3,a=250,$ and ${y}_{0}=150.$ Notice that $a>{y}_{0},$ which is required for inhibited growth. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited growth. In particular, the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=3(250-y),\\,y(0)=400,\n$$\n\ndoes *not* represent inhibited growth model because ${y}_{0}=400>a=250.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124712 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89b993a9-4262-511e-b44b-5bfc2baded85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121292} {:answer/id #uuid "92ea1b9a-48f6-5f5a-970a-360e074d7cb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$21$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121293} {:answer/id #uuid "ded23566-a047-57a7-a52e-d55db3b390cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$17$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121294} {:answer/id #uuid "6205c386-c28f-5b02-994f-a140a082b1b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$45$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121295} {:answer/id #uuid "6c271daf-0958-59ce-9182-e17b45a13ab1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$30$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c271daf-0958-59ce-9182-e17b45a13ab1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$30$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121296} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0cf405c-ac0d-51e2-8a17-9653fef2ee4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "754ac309-f23b-53d4-8c3a-d4c7dd3dd38f" :question/math-academy-id "q-15370" :question/problem "A University Campus has a total of $1\\,500$ students.\nAt the beginning of the new semester at the University, flu affected $300$ students. After $10$ days, the number of students affected by the flu increases to $500.$ To the nearest day, how long does it take for the flu-stricken campus population to reach $800$ students?\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of students affected by the flu, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time, in days.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the campus population affected by the flu, $P(t),$ changes is proportional to the difference between the total campus population and its current population affected. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the total population of the campus, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=1\\,500$ students, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(1\\,500-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{1\\,500-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{1\\,500-P} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1\\,500-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $1\\,500-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,500-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 1\\,500-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 1\\,500-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =1\\,500-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial campus population affected by the flu was $300$ students. So, we apply the initial condition $P(0)=300,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =300 \\\\ 1\\,500-K{e}^{0} & =300 \\\\ 1\\,500-K & =300 \\\\ K & =1\\,200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=1\\,500-1\\,200{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $10$ days, the campus population affected by flu was $500$ students. So, applying the condition $P(10)=500,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =500 \\\\ 1\\,500-1\\,200{e}^{-10k} & =500 \\\\ 1\\,000 & =1\\,200{e}^{-10k} \\\\ {e}^{-10k} & =\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ -10k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{6}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when $800$ students are affected by the flu. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =800 \\\\ 1\\,500-1\\,200{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =800 \\\\ 700 & =1\\,200{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{7}{12} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{12}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{12}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that $800$ students will be affected by the flu after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{12})}{-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{6})}=29.562…≈30\\,\\text{days},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c487e02-f26a-56aa-b7c5-bc5d1ca412e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-k(150+N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321178} {:answer/id #uuid "683c075e-6ddc-5536-b7a3-c5784c6694e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=kN(150-N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321179} {:answer/id #uuid "dc297bd0-0a39-50b2-bd9f-fe4520631b84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-k(150-N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321180} {:answer/id #uuid "fb52132d-5dcd-5e01-8089-3e69edc3bccc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k{N}^{2}(150-N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321181} {:answer/id #uuid "f6654b41-f50b-5db7-983c-da59dc98c8aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k(150-N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6654b41-f50b-5db7-983c-da59dc98c8aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k(150-N)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321182} :answer-field/id #uuid "db12b224-d872-5232-af52-551132bbf28b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "100642f2-5b37-581c-b6bb-cef2fcf7d983" :question/math-academy-id "q-335135" :question/problem "A wildlife manager noted that the growth of the number of deer in a protected forest is directly proportional to the difference between the forest’s maximum deer population and the current population. Let $N(t)$ be the deer population, measured in number of deer, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum deer population that the forest can support is $150$ deer, which differential equation could be used to model the forest’s deer population? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the deer population $N(t)$ of the forest changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum deer population the forest can support and the current deer population. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-N),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum deer population the forest can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=150\\text{ deer},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k(150-N).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c98f81a1-56d4-5e26-bb4d-810ad98de381" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(100-T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321185} {:answer/id #uuid "c023f6c2-c807-555b-ace6-f7d5d9bf9288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(30-T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321186} {:answer/id #uuid "62983bd2-81f7-5b29-b872-3282859bf573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(60-T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321187} {:answer/id #uuid "ab1f1522-91c4-5a8b-94c6-2736e1f48ed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(95-T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321188} {:answer/id #uuid "59433e00-c088-57f7-8813-b8d07339c866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(950-T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab1f1522-91c4-5a8b-94c6-2736e1f48ed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(95-T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321188} :answer-field/id #uuid "f55e52d6-de0c-5f14-a216-0618ad1d3457" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ada01d1e-eba3-5928-91b8-5bffc1a1234f" :question/math-academy-id "q-335142" :question/problem "A tutoring center currently has $10$ enrolled students. The number of students in the center $T(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in months. If the maximum capacity of the tutoring center is $95$ students, which differential equation could be used to model the student population of the center? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Since the number of students in the center obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of students changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of students in the center. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-T),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the tutoring center, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=95$ students, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k(95-T).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321190 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61e1cd94-0168-537b-9253-806228ba636d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321192} {:answer/id #uuid "6258d3ee-665e-517c-892c-53b9d2e82a18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$19$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321193} {:answer/id #uuid "6507e808-0c0b-53ab-83f4-e951283a1a8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321194} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9f460f-a72b-51cf-98a5-9f463a191fe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321195} {:answer/id #uuid "b080d755-4f53-5aaa-b708-48dd41959f72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d9f460f-a72b-51cf-98a5-9f463a191fe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321195} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec52a582-0b36-573b-9119-43c61aefe9f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cad98f12-8da4-54c8-87ad-6af552687ea0" :question/math-academy-id "q-335280" :question/problem "A can of soda is taken out of a refrigerator and left in a room that stays at a constant temperature of $20{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ The soda’s temperature $T(t)$ (in degrees Celsius) can be modeled by the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(20-T),\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\ge 0$ is the time, measured in minutes, and $k>0$ is a constant. The initial temperature of the soda is $8{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and after $5$ minutes, the soda has warmed to $12{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the soda to reach $16{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}?$" :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the can is modeled by the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(20-T).\n$$\n\nWe separate variables and integrate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{20-T} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{20-T} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|20-T| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $20-T>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20-T & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 20-T & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 20-T & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =20-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial temperature was $8{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=8,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =20-K{e}^{0} \\\\ 8 & =20-K \\\\ K & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=20-12{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $5$ minutes, the temperature of the soda was $12{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ So, applying the condition $T(5)=12,$  we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =20-12{e}^{-5k} \\\\ 8 & =12{e}^{-5k} \\\\ {e}^{-5k} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -5k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$  be the time when the soda can reaches $16{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =16 \\\\ 20-12{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =16 \\\\ 4 & =12{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the value of $k,$ we get:\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})}{-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})}=13.547…≈14\\text{ }\\text{minutes},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321197 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bffc4b47-de4e-552a-81b4-6adec40f886f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$570$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321199} {:answer/id #uuid "c4578f9e-b258-5042-b524-dde82d917182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$560$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321200} {:answer/id #uuid "54511ca5-bc2d-5ea2-bdc1-07a9c508099f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$577$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321201} {:answer/id #uuid "66012f87-f092-5ae2-84e1-b8c4819eb86d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$550$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321202} {:answer/id #uuid "22041062-30ce-51ce-9748-f3e2cadeebf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$590$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54511ca5-bc2d-5ea2-bdc1-07a9c508099f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$577$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321201} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea96f7c0-be13-5f40-994f-df9e4b8563dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321198}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ee0a87d3-d3e9-539a-9074-68a374d85d6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-335590" :question/problem "A small bakery produces loaves of bread. The owner knows that the bakery can produce a maximum of $600$ loaves a day; however, current production is below this maximum. Since she started monitoring, the bakers produced $480$ loaves and $500$ loaves on days $4$ and $8,$ respectively. How many loaves do they produce on day $40?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the bread production $E(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the bread production $E(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum possible bread production and its current bread production. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(a-E),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum production of the bakery, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=600$ loaves, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(600-E).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dE}{600-E} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dE}{600-E} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|600-E| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $600-E>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $E(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}600-E & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 600-E & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 600-E & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ E & =600-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on day $4,$ the bread production was $480$ loaves. So, applying the condition $E(4)=480,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(4) & =480 \\\\ 600-K{e}^{-4k} & =480 \\\\ K{e}^{-4k} & =120.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on day $8,$ the bread production was $500$ loaves. So, applying the condition $E(8)=500,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(8) & =500 \\\\ 600-K{e}^{-8k} & =500 \\\\ K{e}^{-8k} & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-4k}}{K{e}^{-8k}} & =\\frac{120}{100} \\\\ {e}^{4k} & =\\frac{6}{5} \\\\ 4k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{6}{5}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{6}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-4\\cdot (1/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)} & =120 \\\\ K & =120{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)} \\\\ K & =144\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $E(t):$\n\n$$\nE(t)=600-144{e}^{-(t/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)}\n$$\n\nFinally, the number of loaves baked on day $t=40$ is\n\n$$\nE(40)=600-144{e}^{-(40/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)}≈576.743…≈577\\,\\text{loaves},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321204 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7aa49a75-0f3f-550f-9d46-14ceefd1ae98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$25\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321206} {:answer/id #uuid "2fe184fd-190b-5065-afb0-8cb5d9f91f50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321207} {:answer/id #uuid "036c91bd-c4ac-56f3-a774-852c33e33e1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321208} {:answer/id #uuid "8610abfa-91bd-509e-86c0-616b55578bba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321209} {:answer/id #uuid "39fb7d14-1be7-50f9-9cec-79cdebaf4c69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fe184fd-190b-5065-afb0-8cb5d9f91f50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321207} :answer-field/id #uuid "002cce17-781a-5345-9b29-bcfe6067a58e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "44cded13-6bc7-52ea-8fcc-d6fe583c3d07" :question/math-academy-id "q-335619" :question/problem "A national park is monitoring the number of people who have downloaded a new park app. The system can support a maximum of $800$ users, but the total is still below this limit. Since the park began tracking downloads, the number of users reached $620$ on day $3$ and $720$ on day $9.$ On what day will the number of users reach $760?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of users, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of app users $P(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum population and its current population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of users the system can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=800$ users, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(800-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{800-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{800-P} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|800-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $800-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}800-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 800-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 800-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =800-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on day $3,$ the number of users was $620.$ So, applying the condition $P(3)=620,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =620 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{-3k} & =620 \\\\ K{e}^{-3k} & =180.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on day $9,$ the number of users was $720.$ So, applying the condition $P(9)=720,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(9) & =720 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{-9k} & =720 \\\\ K{e}^{-9k} & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-3k}}{K{e}^{-9k}} & =\\frac{180}{80} \\\\ {e}^{6k} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 6k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-3\\cdot (1/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9/4)} & =180 \\\\ K & =180{e}^{(1/2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9/4)} \\\\ K & =180\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\ K & =180\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ K & =270\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=800-270{e}^{-(t/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9/4)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $d$  be the day when the app reaches $760$ users. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(d) & =760 \\\\ 800-270{e}^{-kd} & =760 \\\\ 40 & =270{e}^{-kd} \\\\ {e}^{-kd} & =\\frac{40}{270} \\\\ {e}^{-kd} & =\\frac{4}{27} \\\\ -kd & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{27}) \\\\ d & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{27}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the number of users will be $760$ on day\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{27})}{\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{4})}=14.1285…≈14,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321211 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab8e90b6-fce6-5ee2-b02e-500625fcfa43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-k(240+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321213} {:answer/id #uuid "c2136666-ccbf-5a84-819d-9cde72bc69c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(240-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321214} {:answer/id #uuid "fe7a382a-7675-5412-a0e0-d12511823213" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k{P}^{2}(240-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321215} {:answer/id #uuid "96dfdd7b-f1f8-5f03-a1ff-f82c87a56449" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-k(240-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321216} {:answer/id #uuid "acf34fc2-5f67-54c3-b84e-feb6b84400cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=kP(240-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2136666-ccbf-5a84-819d-9cde72bc69c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(240-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321214} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef757241-ee70-5d27-aab6-5430e965c594" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c175d1ed-0a34-56b3-8e25-2e8fcbb6959b" :question/math-academy-id "q-344219" :question/problem "An engineer observed that the growth of the number of solar panels installed at a facility is directly proportional to the difference between the facility’s maximum number of solar panels and the current number installed. Let $P(t)$ be the number of solar panels, measured in panels, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum number of solar panels the facility can support is $240$ panels, which differential equation could be used to model the number of solar panels installed? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of solar panels $P(t)$ installed changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum number of solar panels the facility can support and the current number of solar panels. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of solar panels the facility can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=240\\text{ panels},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(240-P).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4b81586-a95a-5ba1-9b07-0ff42a761c94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(35-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321220} {:answer/id #uuid "c1820a19-d6c7-546c-be14-2659cb5ea0d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(25-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321221} {:answer/id #uuid "ea7f036b-a0bc-5f88-993f-fda36a7407a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(80-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321222} {:answer/id #uuid "bca3e1a4-f164-57ff-bf5e-fa22911d7193" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(18-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321223} {:answer/id #uuid "455f95fc-a0e0-5f92-b6d1-6aaefb9a3204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(50-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "455f95fc-a0e0-5f92-b6d1-6aaefb9a3204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(50-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321224} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfc39c9b-6ab2-5714-87ee-69685dcf93ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5760f0d6-29ea-595e-85a7-9ba740a89ded" :question/math-academy-id "q-344228" :question/problem "A laboratory incubator initially contains $18$ bacterial cultures. The number of cultures in the incubator $B(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in days. If the maximum capacity of the incubator is $50$ cultures, which differential equation could be used to model the number of cultures? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Since the number of cultures in the incubator obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of cultures changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of cultures in the incubator. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-B),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the incubator, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=50$ cultures, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k(50-B).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83d24472-171d-5227-9085-a6ac16e226fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(1000-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321227} {:answer/id #uuid "53f10987-9fa4-5afc-9ffe-13c4e23405fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(320-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321228} {:answer/id #uuid "c401632e-0b15-5737-ae47-e5f17c8f1500" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(1500-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321229} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d07f58-2f80-59b2-9d63-a0dd69fb7e4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(500-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321230} {:answer/id #uuid "3489e363-3a15-59d2-8f35-f5a45874ffae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(800-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83d24472-171d-5227-9085-a6ac16e226fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(1000-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321227} :answer-field/id #uuid "b28ea96b-7627-5164-88ef-c158e3bf07df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa2692ef-2f14-5520-ae8c-62a6f866286f" :question/math-academy-id "q-344233" :question/problem "A data center initially contains $320$ computers. The number of computers in the data center $C(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in months. If the maximum capacity of the data center is $1000$ computers, which differential equation could be used to model the number of computers? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Since the number of computers in the data center obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of computers changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of computers in the data center. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-C),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the data center, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=1000$ computers, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=k(1000-C).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ade69467-c229-509d-9bdc-b28935b2d1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Growth Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3334336b-8b04-5027-8ba4-131c866179b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=22P-19\\,734,\\,P(0)=20\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321234} {:answer/id #uuid "ca7145a4-4841-54fc-8e22-e8f1b6d6d12d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=1\\,250-0.005P,\\,P(0)=270\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321235} {:answer/id #uuid "96bdaaba-375b-507d-b9b3-d9118971dc85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=690-0.003P,\\,P(0)=200\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321236} {:answer/id #uuid "18cd7797-b070-59bc-9f8a-32ac7d55b9f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.02P-875,\\,P(0)=47\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321237} {:answer/id #uuid "290988f4-432e-58d0-bc3b-bb45f73b680f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=5\\,700-0.8P,\\,P(0)=7\\,200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96bdaaba-375b-507d-b9b3-d9118971dc85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=690-0.003P,\\,P(0)=200\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321236} :answer-field/id #uuid "a31bab35-8072-5590-9c13-3a0ef9e98020" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f19b446e-eb9e-54b9-91c3-e10a509f46a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-37176" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited growth problem?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=690-0.003P,\\,P(0)=200\\,000,\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=690-0.003P=0.003(230\\,000-P).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=0.003,a=230\\,000,$ and ${y}_{0}=200\\,000.$ Notice that $a>{y}_{0},$ which is required for inhibited growth. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited growth. In particular, the initial value problems\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=1\\,250-0.005P=0.005(250\\,000-P),\\,P(0)=270\\,000,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=5\\,700-0.8P=0.8(7\\,125-P),\\,P(0)=7\\,200\n$$\n\ndo *not* represent an inhibited growth model because ${y}_{0}=270\\,000>a=250\\,000,$ and ${y}_{0}=7\\,200>a=7\\,125.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ade69467-c229-509d-9bdc-b28935b2d1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Growth Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "673cf19b-ba30-5fc9-b585-447b8c1dc764" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=3P-321,\\,P(0)=120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321241} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c2a96a-42f7-5209-8cba-a046242c8e0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=830-0.8P,\\,P(0)=1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321242} {:answer/id #uuid "4038b0df-b6ab-5727-9810-08a07d57eb35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=7-0.000\\,2P,\\,P(0)=38\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321243} {:answer/id #uuid "d6a08445-d33a-582b-a80e-bd5dbec3d94e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=450-0.03P,\\,P(0)=16\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321244} {:answer/id #uuid "16eed744-d655-5a56-859a-de5a0601f57e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=8\\,200-40P,\\,P(0)=210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1c2a96a-42f7-5209-8cba-a046242c8e0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=830-0.8P,\\,P(0)=1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321242} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6252692-85fa-5a3d-a8d3-ec4ea95998d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f80eaa20-fbfe-5dba-a6c8-edcc5012129a" :question/math-academy-id "q-37177" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited growth problem?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=830-0.8P,\\,P(0)=1\\,000,\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=830-0.8P=0.8(1\\,037.5-P).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=0.8,a=1\\,037.5,$ and ${y}_{0}=1\\,000.$ Notice that $a>{y}_{0},$ which is required for inhibited growth. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited growth. In particular, the initial value problems\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=7-0.000\\,2P=0.000\\,2(35\\,000-P),\\,P(0)=38\\,000,\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=450-0.03P=0.03(15\\,000-P),\\,P(0)=16\\,000,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=8\\,200-40P=40(205-P),\\,P(0)=210\n$$\n\ndo *not* represent an inhibited growth model because ${y}_{0}=38\\,000>a=35\\,000,$ ${y}_{0}=16\\,000>a=15\\,000,$ and ${y}_{0}=210>a=205.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ade69467-c229-509d-9bdc-b28935b2d1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Growth Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd76e344-9cc5-5e4f-b6bb-25d2e5373fe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=12\\,510-18P,\\,P(0)=690" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321248} {:answer/id #uuid "79f5c0e5-888b-5885-8f9a-109a0df38520" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.5P-1\\,23,\\,P(0)=2\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321249} {:answer/id #uuid "dac94d52-d256-569b-b8b5-9de8af332f00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=9P-6\\,066,\\,P(0)=680" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321250} {:answer/id #uuid "08ff9d27-8424-57e8-b861-0e05afdf7397" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=2\\,580-0.6P,\\,P(0)=4\\,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321251} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ad886b-98d3-5405-acc9-1f28973c2b21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=16\\,256-4P,\\,P(0)=4\\,070" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd76e344-9cc5-5e4f-b6bb-25d2e5373fe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=12\\,510-18P,\\,P(0)=690" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321248} :answer-field/id #uuid "000d9719-34fa-5f66-b960-4e9cd517265b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6960a095-e760-5362-9086-48e7e2e653fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-37180" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited growth problem?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=12\\,510-18P,\\,P(0)=690,\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=12\\,510-18P=18(695-P).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=18,a=695,$ and ${y}_{0}=690.$ Notice that $a>{y}_{0},$ which is required for inhibited growth. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited growth. In particular, the initial value problems\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=16\\,256-4P=4(4\\,064-P),\\,P(0)=4\\,070,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=2\\,580-0.6P=0.6(4\\,300-P),\\,P(0)=4\\,400\n$$\n\ndo *not* represent an inhibited growth model because ${y}_{0}=4\\,070>a=4\\,064,$ and ${y}_{0}=4\\,400>a=4\\,300.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a1fe324-69f6-5f4f-ae16-55a872bd809c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1005$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122933} {:answer/id #uuid "cf0a5949-da89-513e-b03f-ddb7fa6aa7c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$905$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122934} {:answer/id #uuid "0aa71735-2ec4-5cf6-8b7a-35bcbcf4c36b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$850$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122935} {:answer/id #uuid "1c80e5d3-c034-55b8-81b4-61017785fef5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$942$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122936} {:answer/id #uuid "50eb4fa4-b748-5dfe-8015-56d79f9b0e57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$986$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf0a5949-da89-513e-b03f-ddb7fa6aa7c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$905$ students" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122934} :answer-field/id #uuid "f81c5408-efc5-5c6c-9065-f0d086ce6d1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d1a929a-56b1-5075-bd38-17c32dd77741" :question/math-academy-id "q-37400" :question/problem "The population of students $P(t)$ in a school can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.016(2\\,500-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in years. If the initial population of the school is $800$ students, calculate the population after $4$ years. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We're told that the population in the school is modeled by the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.016(2\\,500-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{2\\,500-P} & =0.016\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{2\\,500-P} & =\\int 0.016\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2\\,500-P| & =0.016t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $2\\,500-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2500-P & ={e}^{-0.016t-C} \\\\ 2500-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-0.016t} \\\\ 2,500-P & =K{e}^{-0.016t} \\\\ P & =2\\,500-K{e}^{-0.016t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial population of the school was $800$ students. So, we apply the initial condition $P(0)=800,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}800 & =2\\,500-K{e}^{0} \\\\ 800 & =2\\,500-K \\\\ K & =1\\,700\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nP(t)=2\\,500-1\\,700{e}^{-0.016t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the population in the school after $t=4$ years is\n\n$$\nP(4)=2\\,500-1\\,700{e}^{-0.016\\cdot 4}≈905\\,\\text{students},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}]}
